+# The Daily Grind
+
+A static coffee website served by a dependency-free Node.js server. Requires Node.js 22 or newer.
+
+```sh
+npm start
+npm run check
+```
+
+The server listens on port 11222 by default; configure `PORT` and `HOST` as needed. Run this server rather than a generic static file server: security headers and logout handling are HTTP behaviors implemented in `server.mjs`.
+
+## Privacy architecture
+
+- No accounts, customer database, collection forms, analytics, cookies, browser storage, or fingerprinting. Existing menu/account/order links are placeholders; these services are not implemented.
+- Requests are processed in memory to deliver static resources. No application access logs, request bodies, addresses, URL queries, identifiers, or violation reports are retained. Request bodies are not parsed, reflected, or stored.
+- Only explicitly allowlisted files are public; source, tests, configuration, and repository files cannot be downloaded.
+- All assets are served locally. The existing stock coffee photo was downloaded from [Unsplash](https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1495474472287-4d71bcdd2085?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1350&q=80); visitors do not contact Unsplash. System fonts avoid external font requests.
+- `/privacy-policy` explains current collection and retention honestly. `/logout` requires a confirmed same-origin POST before sending `Clear-Site-Data`. It requests browser data removal; it does not terminate a server session because none exists. Cookie clearing can affect sibling subdomains: deploy on a dedicated site if sibling applications use cookies.
+
+## Deployment requirements
+
+Terminate TLS at a trusted reverse proxy or hosting edge, redirect public HTTP to HTTPS there, and restrict direct access to the Node HTTP backend. Never treat untrusted forwarded headers as proof of TLS. The development server itself does not provide TLS.
+
+Preserve the server's response headers at the proxy/CDN. CSP blocks all scripts, embeds, network connections, and external assets; CSS and images are allowed only from this origin. Styles were extracted from HTML so no `unsafe-inline` exception is necessary. Responses use `no-store`, `no-referrer`, restrictive Permissions Policy, clickjacking protection, MIME protection, and origin isolation headers. No CORS permissions are granted. Fetch Metadata blocks cross-site resource requests while allowing normal incoming page navigation, with a compatibility fallback when browsers omit the headers.
+
+HSTS starts at `max-age=300` to avoid long-lived mistakes during HTTPS rollout. After confirming production TLS, increase to `31536000`; enable subdomains/preload only after auditing every affected hostname. HSTS and Clear-Site-Data require a secure context and browser support. Clear-Site-Data has a browser-settings fallback described on the page.
+
+Disable request/access logging at the hosting edge and upstream services, or explicitly define a minimal purpose, redaction, retention period, and deletion process. Review provider contracts, subprocessors, backups, and incident access. The application cannot enforce provider retention or certify their practices. Do not put secrets in URLs. No telemetry collector is enabled: CSP reports can contain sensitive URLs or script samples; adding one requires a separate privacy review with redaction, limits, and retention.
+
+## Third-party and data-flow review
+
+Current inventory (2026-10-19):
+
+| Component | Visitor data sent | Retention / mitigation |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Application server | Connection and requested resource, transiently | No persisted access logs or visitor records |
+| Hosting/TLS provider | Network metadata required for delivery | Deployment owner must configure and verify retention |
+| Unsplash stock image | None from visitors | Image served locally; only the developer download contacts Unsplash |
+| Third-party scripts, APIs, fonts, embeds, dependencies | None | CSP denies them; automated audit guards the inventory |
+
+Run `npm run check` for every change (also enforced by GitHub Actions). It runs a source/dependency audit and HTTP integration tests, including security headers, asset delivery, private-file exposure, logout, and cross-origin restrictions. The source audit is a conservative regression guard, not a substitute for reviewing dynamic network behavior if scripts are introduced. No browser verification is required for the current script-free site.
+
+Review the inventory on every dependency/integration change and at least quarterly. Before introducing a service, record its owner, exact fields, purpose, legal basis/consent where applicable, destinations/subprocessors, retention/deletion, and user access/correction/export paths. Require necessity before adding fields, prefer guest flows and coarse data, and prohibit repurposing for marketing without fresh consent. Use `__Host-` session cookies with `Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax; Path=/` if authentication is added; never store tokens or personal data in browser storage. Add server-side authorization, input validation, CSRF protection, and sensitive-endpoint rate limits before shipping those features.
+
+Header references: [MDN CSP](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Content-Security-Policy), [MDN Clear-Site-Data](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Clear-Site-Data).
